On Wed, 24 Nov 93 10:16:55 EST you said:
>
>\def\emph#1{{\em #1}\futurelet\next\itcorr}
>\def\itcorr{\ifx\next.\else\if\next,\else\/\fi\fi}
>
>which seems to work well.  Hope this helps.  Of course, this is for
>short (less than one paragraph) passages only.  It absorbs its whole
>argument and is not defined as \long.  For longer arguments, you
>would want to stick with {\em....}.
Now, this is a good solution, I think. For emphasis on more than
one paragraph it is reasonable to suppose that start and end of
the emphasized text will correspond to start and end of a paragraph,
so we don't need italic correction anyhow.
Could a structure like this not be part of standard LaTeX, and
could not the LaTeX book/companion mention this as a preferred
way of emphasizing?

